uid "ed0ba07f-9ec1-55d7-ab0e-5b909c4fa1a0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535920 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186535622 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535920 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535920 #edb/ref 1077 "field-2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535920 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186535622 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535920 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f288405a-29b1-5b71-8025-b48b0179b10a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535931 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186535843 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535931 #edb/ref 1069 "q-280382" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535931 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535931 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following parabola:\n\n$$\nx=15{t}^{2},\\,y=3t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of the parabola is\n\n${y}^{2}=$\n{{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535931 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "117d2bbe-700d-5500-84d8-cda2c172b75c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535931 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =\\frac{x}{15},\\,t=\\frac{y}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=\\frac{x}{15}$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & =\\frac{x}{15} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =\\frac{3}{5}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian equation of the parabola is ${y}^{2}=\\frac{3}{5}x.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535946 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186535871 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535946 #edb/ref 1069 "q-106414" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535946 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535946 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/89/8934e5b6135b97b845dad9a6b87056d2c228fd2424d3a72141df694d46ccf7c0.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535946 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "476be737-90ba-5bfe-8a20-c650319e8b3b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535946 #edb/ref 1074 "The diagram shows that the parabola is vertical, has its vertex at $(0,0),$ and passes through the point $(-6,12).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a vertical parabola  that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx=2pt,\\,y=p{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(-6,12),$ we can substitute $x=-6$ and $y=12$ into the standard parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-6=2pt \\\\ 12=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6 & =2pt \\\\ -3 & =pt \\\\ t & =-\\frac{3}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12 & =p{(-\\frac{3}{p})}^{2} \\\\ 12 & =p\\cdot \\frac{9}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ 12 & =\\frac{9}{p} \\\\ p & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx=2(\\frac{3}{4})t,\\,y=(\\frac{3}{4}){t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{3}{2}t,\\,y=\\frac{3}{4}{t}^{2}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535947 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186535849 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535947 #edb/ref 1069 "q-84735" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535947 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535947 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2},\\,y=-t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535947 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4d316b19-46f8-547d-95ad-bbdabb50ffb3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535947 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =-2x,\\,t=-y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=-2x$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =-2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian equation of the parabola is ${y}^{2}=-2x.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535952 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186535879 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535952 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186535920 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535952 #edb/ref 1069 "q-280220" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535952 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535952 #edb/ref 1071 "Determine a set of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ that generate a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ and which passes through the point $(-8,8).$ Express your answer in the form $x=2pt,y=p{t}^{2},$ where $p$ is a constant.\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}},\n$\\,y=$\n{{field-2}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535952 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5daa6476-7e34-5e5c-afc2-27c877b6a525" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535952 #edb/ref 1074 "The parametric equations of a vertical parabola  that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx=2pt,\\,y=p{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(-8,8),$ we can substitute $x=-8$ and $y=8$ into the standard parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-8=2pt \\\\ 8=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-8 & =2pt \\\\ t & =-\\frac{4}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8 & =p{(-\\frac{4}{p})}^{2} \\\\ 8 & =p\\cdot \\frac{16}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ 8 & =\\frac{16}{p} \\\\ p & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx=2(2)t,\\,y=(2){t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx=4t,\\,y=2{t}^{2}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535964 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186535896 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535964 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186535915 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535964 #edb/ref 1069 "q-280225" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535964 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535964 #edb/ref 1071 "Determine a set of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ that generate the horizontal parabola given by the Cartesian equation ${y}^{2}=-24x.$ Express your answer in the form $x=p{t}^{2},y=2pt,$ where $p$ is a constant.\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}},\n$\\,y=$\n{{field-2}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535964 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8855f85e-af22-5017-b471-837c5222e69b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535964 #edb/ref 1074 "The parametric equations of a horizontal parabola ${y}^{2}=4px$ that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx=p{t}^{2},\\,y=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nComparing the given Cartesian equation with the general case ${y}^{2}=4px$ and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4px & =-24x \\\\ 4p & =-24 \\\\ p & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx=(-6){t}^{2},\\,y=2(-6)t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx=-6{t}^{2},\\,y=-12t.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535971 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186535917 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535971 #edb/ref 1069 "q-106443" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535971 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535971 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2},\\,y=-\\frac{1}{6}t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535971 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9466ac77-f4bf-5bd1-8cef-81367e73b3b6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535971 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =-4x,\\,t=-6y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =36{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=-4x$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}36{y}^{2} & =-4x \\\\ {y}^{2} & =-\\frac{1}{9}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian equation of the parabola is ${y}^{2}=-\\frac{1}{9}x.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535987 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186535853 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535987 #edb/ref 1069 "q-84714" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535987 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535987 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the horizontal parabola given by the Cartesian equation ${y}^{2}=-10x?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535987 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bf998376-0b5d-5f0e-ac72-6bbc0864becd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535987 #edb/ref 1074 "The parametric equations of a horizontal parabola ${y}^{2}=4px$ that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx=p{t}^{2},\\,y=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nComparing the given Cartesian equation with the general case ${y}^{2}=4px$ and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4px & =-10x \\\\ 4p & =-10 \\\\ p & =-\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx=(-\\frac{5}{2}){t}^{2},\\,y=2(-\\frac{5}{2})t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{5}{2}{t}^{2},\\,y=-5t.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
